{
  "verdict": "GO_WITH_CONDITIONS",
  "strongest_argument": "The migration removes an extension-owned reimplementation of tree accessibility, keyboard handling, focus, layout, and actions that has already produced a user-visible interaction regression undetectable by its unit and static checks. The primary justification is correctness and reduced maintenance risk, not performance.",
  "performance_case_real": {
    "answer": "partly",
    "reasoning": "No: a TreeDataProvider will not make the host-side scan faster, so it should not be credited with fixing empty-tree startup. Removing webview initialization may save some time, but that must be measured and is unlikely to explain the reported symptom if the scan dominates. Native lazy children should reduce rendering and DOM work for populated, collapsed trees, so there is a separate and credible populated-tree performance benefit."
  },
  "lazy_children_trade": {
    "good_trade": true,
    "what_would_make_it_bad": "It becomes a bad trade if getChildren performs filesystem work, recomputes or sorts large structures repeatedly, or produces perceptible expansion stalls. It is also bad if the operator routinely expands the entire hierarchy and cumulative expansion time exceeds the current render cost. Keep it as a cheap projection over the in-memory model, cache stable child lists, and reject it if representative p95 expansion latency exceeds roughly 100 ms."
  },
  "density_trade": {
    "acceptable": "operator_must_decide",
    "reasoning": "Putting information in a tooltip does not preserve its operational value for repeated visual scanning. Five independently visible markers becoming one severity icon plus hover-only details is a real regression, and the fourth level can hide session state whenever its ancestors are collapsed. This is not automatically fatal, but it is the principal decision gate and cannot be settled from API capability alone.",
    "mitigations": [
      "Build a native vertical slice with representative rows containing every marker combination, four module actions, and the fourth level before authorizing the full migration.",
      "Run timed side-by-side scan tasks with the operator, measuring missed critical states as well as completion time.",
      "Keep the most actionable or severe descendant state visible on collapsed ancestors rather than relying exclusively on child session icons.",
      "Use a concise, stable description grammar where it improves scanability, but do not overload labels with inaccessible symbol codes.",
      "Verify the four inline actions at the minimum supported view width and provide context-menu or submenu access for actions that overflow."
    ]
  },
  "no_go_conditions": [
    "In a representative side-by-side test, the operator misses any blocked, failed-verification, or migration-required state that was reliably visible in the current view.",
    "Median completion time for agreed daily scan tasks regresses by more than 20%, or the operator must hover rows individually to complete those tasks.",
    "The four frequent module actions cannot remain discoverable and usable at the minimum supported panel width, and moving them to overflow or context menus is rejected by the operator.",
    "Representative p95 child expansion latency exceeds 100 ms after cheap caching, or expanding the normal working set is slower than the current view.",
    "The fourth level makes normal scanning materially worse because useful session status disappears under collapsed ancestors and no acceptable aggregate state fits on the parent.",
    "If faster empty-tree startup is a release requirement, profiling shows the filesystem scan dominates and scan changes remain out of scope. In that case, this migration must not be sold or approved as the startup-performance fix."
  ],
  "plan_critique": [
    {
      "session": 1,
      "problem": "A decision based only on measurements and API inspection will miss the largest risk: loss of simultaneous information density. It may also conflate empty startup, scan time, webview startup, and populated rendering.",
      "fix": "Instrument scan, model assembly, view creation, and first visible render separately. Build the smallest native vertical slice needed to test dense rows, actions, collapsed-state signaling, and the fourth level. Put the migration decision at the end of this session."
    },
    {
      "session": 2,
      "problem": "Building only the provider behind the existing surface validates data adaptation but not the risky native presentation, menu behavior, tooltip usability, stacked-view layout, or operator workflow.",
      "fix": "Create an alternate native view behind a feature flag and implement one complete vertical slice, including commands, menus, tooltips, icons, expansion, and provider contract tests. Keep the existing view available for direct comparison."
    },
    {
      "session": 3,
      "problem": "Switching over, deleting the old renderer, re-homing other surfaces, and rewriting both suites at once removes the rollback path and the behavioral oracle while changing implementation and tests together. New tests can simply encode the new defects.",
      "fix": "Switch behind a flag first, retain the old renderer temporarily, and add native-path tests before deleting anything. Preserve implementation-independent behavioral assertions and adapt the Playwright regression for swallowed clicks and action invocation. Re-home non-tree surfaces as a separate change. Delete the old implementation only after comparison and acceptance."
    },
    {
      "session": 4,
      "problem": "Walking the real repository only after deletion discovers density, expansion, command, and layout failures too late. Combining first representative validation, repairs, final measurement, and release staging creates pressure to accept regressions.",
      "fix": "Move the real-repository walk and pre/post measurements before the old path is deleted. Use this session for soak testing, accessibility and keyboard checks, rollback verification, final cleanup, and release staging."
    }
  ],
  "what_this_panel_might_be_getting_wrong": "The native view may reduce defect and maintenance risk less than expected if the operator's productivity depends heavily on the current at-a-glance encoding. Conversely, the operator may find the fourth level and native consistency clearer than the raw signal-count comparison suggests. Only a representative side-by-side task test can resolve that uncertainty."
}
